WEBVTT

00:00:06.400 --> 00:00:11.910
I share my unstoppable curiosity with you.

00:00:13.990 --> 00:00:17.710
I'm still able to run through the fog...

00:00:17.810 --> 00:00:21.400
...because I know that you're here for me.

00:00:21.500 --> 00:00:29.130
I guess the shape of happiness is different for everyone...

00:00:29.130 --> 00:00:33.470
...however, I know it's boring when I'm alone...

00:00:33.890 --> 00:00:35.350
But, with you...

00:00:35.350 --> 00:00:37.430
...it's like a mystery!

00:00:39.270 --> 00:00:43.020
It's an enchanting future, where...

00:00:43.190 --> 00:00:47.740
...in order for me to be strong, it's always you...

00:00:47.740 --> 00:00:52.120
...that I can continue to depend on, right?

00:00:52.120 --> 00:00:53.700
Just to survive!

00:00:53.870 --> 00:00:55.660
Stumped Q? Don't lose your way!

00:00:55.660 --> 00:00:57.500
Take this moment...

00:00:57.500 --> 00:01:01.120
...to fix your gaze and charge on ahead!

00:01:01.120 --> 00:01:03.330
Eternally, we'll always be together...

00:01:03.330 --> 00:01:05.170
...searching and pursuing...

00:01:05.170 --> 00:01:08.880
...the meaning of it all.

00:01:08.880 --> 00:01:11.010
Truth is nothing but a labyrinth...

00:01:11.010 --> 00:01:12.720
...that we try to continue through...

00:01:12.720 --> 00:01:16.390
...especially when we are hurt.

00:01:16.390 --> 00:01:18.560
I'll take you, so come this way.

00:01:18.560 --> 00:01:20.390
I'm certain that, on that day...

00:01:20.390 --> 00:01:24.310
...I'll see your smile, face to face!

00:01:38.374 --> 00:01:40.076
Wow, what a cool cruiser!

00:01:40.176 --> 00:01:46.249
We're really lucky to be included in
Class A's extra special training.

00:01:46.783 --> 00:01:51.321
The special training will be putting
our detective skills into practice…

00:01:51.521 --> 00:01:55.091
…by inspecting the actual scene
of an unsolved crime.

00:01:55.558 --> 00:01:56.926
Sounds serious, huh?

00:01:57.227 --> 00:02:01.297
Well, we were invited because they've acknowledged
what we've already done.

00:02:01.731 --> 00:02:04.167
I suppose there really isn't anything
to be nervous about.

00:02:06.202 --> 00:02:07.570
Whoa! What's this?

00:02:07.770 --> 00:02:09.472
Do we really get to go on this ship?

00:02:09.706 --> 00:02:11.140
Ah, sea slaters…

00:02:11.407 --> 00:02:14.110
Wow! There are so many!

00:02:14.110 --> 00:02:15.411
Kyuu.

00:02:15.411 --> 00:02:18.881
We're not here for fun and games,
so get serious, will ya?

00:02:19.048 --> 00:02:22.252
Oh boy, are you people sure you can handle this?

00:02:22.585 --> 00:02:25.622
You're going too, Saburoumaru-san?

00:02:25.855 --> 00:02:27.290
Of course I am!

00:02:27.857 --> 00:02:31.361
You Class Q people are as noisy as always.

00:02:31.628 --> 00:02:34.430
And you're as annoying as always.

00:02:34.464 --> 00:02:36.933
Wow! There are so many here too!

00:02:37.533 --> 00:02:40.970
I'll say this now: This is Class A's special training.

00:02:41.337 --> 00:02:44.907
You Class Q people are merely persistent extras.

00:02:45.541 --> 00:02:50.079
You'd better not be dragging Class A down…

00:02:51.781 --> 00:02:53.816
Everyone from Class Q appears to be here, right?

00:02:53.816 --> 00:02:55.118
Yep!

00:02:55.118 --> 00:02:56.552
Alright then, let's go on board.

00:03:00.323 --> 00:03:02.225
Whoa, wait up!

00:03:04.794 --> 00:03:06.629
Everyone's on board.

00:03:26.816 --> 00:03:29.319
So those are the people from Class A?

00:03:29.585 --> 00:03:32.655
Besides that Saburoumaru moron, this
is the first time I've seen any of them.

00:03:32.789 --> 00:03:34.157
Same here.

00:03:34.457 --> 00:03:36.192
These guys are the top of Class A.

00:03:36.426 --> 00:03:39.028
And each of them are in charge of various
cases so they're pretty busy.

00:03:39.829 --> 00:03:41.898
Their resume's are outstanding as well.

00:03:42.899 --> 00:03:44.967
First, the guy on the left with the glasses.

00:03:45.301 --> 00:03:46.869
His name is Gouda Kyousuke.

00:03:47.437 --> 00:03:50.673
He's the so called puzzle king, he swept away the competition in the national puzzle contest.

00:03:52.241 --> 00:03:54.110
The guy behind him is Shishido Takeshi.

00:03:54.911 --> 00:03:58.147
He's already solved four murder cases.

00:03:58.147 --> 00:04:00.049
Four?

00:04:00.049 --> 00:04:01.250
And then, the next one…

00:04:01.351 --> 00:04:02.418
Hey, you guys.

00:04:03.886 --> 00:04:07.290
Instead of looking us up online,
why not introduce yourselves.

00:04:07.824 --> 00:04:11.694
Sneaking around in the shadows and investigating
us sort of makes us feel bad.

00:04:11.728 --> 00:04:14.397
E-Excuse us. That's not what we meant.

00:04:15.431 --> 00:04:17.266
I'm Yukihira Sakurako.

00:04:17.867 --> 00:04:18.735
Nice to meet you.

00:04:19.202 --> 00:04:20.136
Yukihira…

00:04:20.603 --> 00:04:22.538
Don't tell me you're that novelist?

00:04:23.639 --> 00:04:25.041
Is she someone famous?

00:04:25.508 --> 00:04:28.244
She's the high school girl who's
well-known as the author of…

00:04:28.244 --> 00:04:30.179
…several successive best-selling mystery novels…

00:04:30.380 --> 00:04:32.148
…Yukihira Sakurako-san.

00:04:32.749 --> 00:04:34.050
My, you're pretty smart.

00:04:34.617 --> 00:04:36.719
But writing is just a hobby of mine.

00:04:37.253 --> 00:04:39.822
My actual aim is to become a great detective.

00:04:40.390 --> 00:04:41.691
Next up is me.

00:04:42.558 --> 00:04:43.860
He's Shiramine-kun.

00:04:44.260 --> 00:04:45.261
And his specialty is…

00:04:54.971 --> 00:04:57.306
Wow! It's a trick, it's a trick!

00:04:57.807 --> 00:04:59.342
Please call it 'magic'.

00:05:00.109 --> 00:05:03.079
Since we're all going to be training together,
let's become good friends!

00:05:03.279 --> 00:05:04.013
Okay!

00:05:13.289 --> 00:05:14.690
Ah, Katagiri-Sensei.

00:05:14.757 --> 00:05:16.159
Are you bored Kyuu?

00:05:16.359 --> 00:05:19.562
No, I'm all excited, and can't keep still.

00:05:19.862 --> 00:05:22.031
I see. Well, just try your best.

00:05:24.801 --> 00:05:26.702
Ah, there's somebody over there.

00:05:27.336 --> 00:05:29.505
I wonder if he's the same age as me.

00:05:30.873 --> 00:05:34.510
Hey, you over there, are you from Class A, too?

00:05:36.479 --> 00:05:37.980
There's a storm coming.

00:05:39.115 --> 00:05:40.082
A storm?

00:05:41.284 --> 00:05:42.985
Ah, sorry for being so sudden.

00:05:43.286 --> 00:05:45.288
I'm Kyuu, from Class Q.

00:05:45.888 --> 00:05:46.622
And you are?

00:05:47.957 --> 00:05:49.091
I'm Ryuu.

00:05:49.992 --> 00:05:51.194
Amakusa Ryuu.

00:05:52.028 --> 00:05:54.330
Ryan-kun, then? Nice to meet you.

00:05:55.097 --> 00:05:57.500
An island! I can see an island!

00:05:59.101 --> 00:06:01.437
So that's where we'll be doing the extra training…

00:06:01.838 --> 00:06:03.172
I'm looking forward to it.

00:06:04.106 --> 00:06:05.808
Don't you know anything about it?

00:06:06.776 --> 00:06:08.177
About that island…

00:06:08.411 --> 00:06:10.146
None, nothing at all.

00:06:10.680 --> 00:06:12.148
Do you know anything about it Ryuu?

00:06:13.049 --> 00:06:15.418
That island's called Kirisaki Island.

00:06:15.651 --> 00:06:16.853
Kirisaki…?

00:06:17.019 --> 00:06:17.887
You mean…

00:06:19.355 --> 00:06:21.324
…when you slash someone with
a sword or something?

00:06:21.324 --> 00:06:23.826
No.

00:06:23.826 --> 00:06:26.295
This one is written 'the mist blossoms'.

00:06:26.829 --> 00:06:30.299
It's an unpopulated island which is always shrouded by mists up to 2km thick

00:06:31.434 --> 00:06:35.037
But it seems that it used to be populated.

00:06:35.872 --> 00:06:37.974
Kirisaki Island, huh?

00:06:48.885 --> 00:06:50.052
I'm the first!

00:06:50.419 --> 00:06:52.855
We're finally here at Kirisaki Island!

00:06:52.889 --> 00:06:54.857
Aren't you a bit too excited?

00:06:54.857 --> 00:06:56.192
Geez.

00:06:56.192 --> 00:06:57.693
I knew it, we're out of range!

00:06:57.960 --> 00:07:00.162
This way I won't be able to connect to the Internet!

00:07:00.396 --> 00:07:02.765
You depend too much on that thing.

00:07:02.765 --> 00:07:04.902
Umm…

00:07:06.602 --> 00:07:07.169
What is it?

00:07:08.571 --> 00:07:10.106
Uh, no, nothing…

00:07:10.573 --> 00:07:11.707
Is something wrong, Megu?

00:07:11.941 --> 00:07:13.809
No, it's nothing.

00:07:14.176 --> 00:07:17.013
I think I'm just a little tired.

00:07:21.751 --> 00:07:24.053
Here's where we'll be staying.

00:07:25.454 --> 00:07:26.689
What is this place?

00:07:26.956 --> 00:07:28.791
It looks like an asylum.

00:07:28.791 --> 00:07:30.726
Hey…

00:07:30.726 --> 00:07:31.928
Who's that?

00:07:32.528 --> 00:07:37.099
Who knows? He doesn't seem like an outsider,
but I've never seen his face before.

00:07:37.633 --> 00:07:39.101
Then, just who is…?

00:07:39.468 --> 00:07:40.736
Hey, you!

00:07:41.404 --> 00:07:43.306
Yeah, I'm talking to you.

00:07:43.606 --> 00:07:45.675
Just who are you?

00:07:46.208 --> 00:07:49.545
You don't look like one of those
clowns from Class Q but…

00:07:52.348 --> 00:07:54.116
I don't know who or what you are,

00:07:54.116 --> 00:07:56.719
but if you're not from Class A,
you're merely a lowly extra.

00:07:57.053 --> 00:07:59.956
You'd better not be getting in our way.

00:08:00.022 --> 00:08:01.057
Got it?

00:08:14.570 --> 00:08:16.472
I wonder whose portraits those are.

00:08:16.906 --> 00:08:19.742
That's Holmes. It's from "The Final Problem."

00:08:21.444 --> 00:08:24.680
This is from Agatha Christie's "And
Then There Were None."

00:08:25.815 --> 00:08:27.316
And this from Ellery Queen.

00:08:27.516 --> 00:08:31.287
He's the schoolmaster Andrew Ban from
"The Egyptian Cross Mystery."

00:08:32.388 --> 00:08:34.824
Well now, let me show you around the premises.

00:08:37.860 --> 00:08:39.195
This is the kitchen.

00:08:39.528 --> 00:08:43.132
The building may be old, but all the
kitchenware is in good condition.

00:08:47.203 --> 00:08:49.338
The ingredients are nice and fresh too.

00:08:50.006 --> 00:08:53.409
Whoever's able to cook and wants
to help will be appreciated.

00:08:53.476 --> 00:08:55.111
Ah, I can!

00:08:55.511 --> 00:08:57.079
I can too, if it's cup ramen…

00:08:57.246 --> 00:08:58.881
Cup ramen isn't cooking.

00:09:03.085 --> 00:09:03.753
What's wrong?

00:09:04.720 --> 00:09:07.556
A rat! Just now, over there, I saw a rat…

00:09:07.823 --> 00:09:09.291
What, it's just a rat.

00:09:09.625 --> 00:09:13.462
It's obvious there would be rats; this
building was built before the war.

00:09:13.796 --> 00:09:17.800
Are you really a student of the Detective
Academy, afraid of a mere rat like that?

00:09:18.100 --> 00:09:19.402
I-I'm sorry.

00:09:20.069 --> 00:09:21.971
Now let me show you all to your rooms.

00:09:22.338 --> 00:09:25.241
Once you've dropped off your luggage,
please assemble in the seminar room.

00:09:26.308 --> 00:09:27.643
That really gave me a scare.

00:09:28.177 --> 00:09:29.712
Don't let it bother you.

00:09:36.652 --> 00:09:42.291
This training camp will be personally
supervised by the headmaster of our
academy, Dan Morihiko-Sensei.

00:09:43.025 --> 00:09:47.163
I'm Maki Shintarou; I'll be part of his support staff.

00:09:47.997 --> 00:09:50.866
I'm employed by the Detective Academy
as a medical examiner.

00:09:51.667 --> 00:09:53.269
And now, Dan-Sensei.

00:09:55.938 --> 00:09:58.207
Because we have first-year students here this time…

00:09:58.507 --> 00:10:01.644
I'll briefly mention the objective of
this training camp once again.

00:10:02.578 --> 00:10:06.248
This extra-curricular training camp is to allow
you to demonstrate your reasoning skills.

00:10:06.248 --> 00:10:09.218
…at the actual site where an unsolved
case happened in the past.

00:10:09.418 --> 00:10:12.521
And I'd like you all to solve the case and
give a full account of what happened.

00:10:14.290 --> 00:10:18.094
And now, I declare the DDS special extra-curricular
training camp officially open.

00:10:20.096 --> 00:10:24.100
Getting to the point, I assume you
have all heard of Jack the Ripper?

00:10:24.333 --> 00:10:26.335
Jack… The Ripper.

00:10:27.169 --> 00:10:31.040
You mean those horrible murder cases that
happened a long time ago in England?

00:10:31.273 --> 00:10:40.149
Correct. In the year 1888, in the slum district of
London's East End, there were a series of murders.

00:10:40.616 --> 00:10:44.754
It was one of the most famous and most
mysterious homicide cases in history.

00:10:47.490 --> 00:10:53.028
Wearing a bowler hat and a long trench-coat,
the criminal called himself Jack the Ripper.

00:10:53.362 --> 00:10:58.534
That name came about because he used a huge
knife and hacked his victims into pieces.

00:10:59.068 --> 00:11:03.672
He composed the letters about his crimes and
sent them to the police as if to taunt them.

00:11:04.006 --> 00:11:07.977
In the end, the case remained unsolved.

00:11:08.611 --> 00:11:14.083
And about 50-odd years back, in the
very building that we are in now…

00:11:14.450 --> 00:11:18.687
…the same sort of mutilation murders occurred.

00:11:19.188 --> 00:11:21.924
In this very building…

00:11:22.558 --> 00:11:24.193
Mutilation murders…

00:11:24.894 --> 00:11:25.661
That's right.

00:11:26.729 --> 00:11:29.765
It was during the war when a criminal
who also called himself…

00:11:29.965 --> 00:11:32.034
…Jack the Ripper committed a series of murders.

00:11:32.268 --> 00:11:35.004
After three victims were hacked to death…

00:11:35.404 --> 00:11:38.374
…the only key was left in the
room with the last victim.

00:11:38.941 --> 00:11:41.877
In a closed room behind two locked doors.

00:11:42.077 --> 00:11:44.680
The criminal disappeared without a trace.

00:11:45.648 --> 00:11:47.983
A closed room behind two locked doors?

00:11:55.991 --> 00:11:58.327
You must all be tired from the long journey.

00:11:58.994 --> 00:12:02.832
I'll tell you the remaining details
tomorrow at the crime scene.

00:12:03.632 --> 00:12:08.304
Everyone, you should just relax
and enjoy yourselves today.

00:12:13.509 --> 00:12:14.977
What a violent storm.

00:12:18.447 --> 00:12:21.450
While this keeps going, we won't
be able to leave the island.

00:12:22.117 --> 00:12:23.786
Even if anything were to happen…

00:12:33.829 --> 00:12:36.332
What's up with that Amakusa Ryuu guy?

00:12:36.866 --> 00:12:39.568
He's really weird and he gives me the creeps.

00:12:40.502 --> 00:12:41.604
Is something up, Megu?

00:12:41.737 --> 00:12:42.872
Not really.

00:12:43.405 --> 00:12:45.140
That Amakusa-san…

00:12:45.207 --> 00:12:47.676
I feel like I've seen him somewhere before.

00:12:48.444 --> 00:12:51.280
But, with my photographic memory…

00:12:51.313 --> 00:12:54.049
…it's not like I could have forgotten
if I'd met him before.

00:12:54.617 --> 00:12:57.219
I can't forget even if I want to…

00:12:57.920 --> 00:12:58.654
Megu.

00:12:59.154 --> 00:13:00.189
Could that mean…

00:13:03.292 --> 00:13:04.660
…you're smitten by his looks?

00:13:04.727 --> 00:13:06.528
Stop saying such weird things!

00:13:06.762 --> 00:13:08.030
Then why are you turning red?

00:13:08.364 --> 00:13:09.064
Idiot!

00:13:09.431 --> 00:13:10.799
Let's go make dinner.

00:13:10.866 --> 00:13:12.201
Ah, wait up!

00:14:02.017 --> 00:14:03.485
The kitchen knives are gone?

00:14:03.719 --> 00:14:07.756
Yeah. When I looked before, there
were three of them here.

00:14:07.890 --> 00:14:08.958
I'm sure of it.

00:14:09.224 --> 00:14:10.893
I wonder who took them…

00:14:11.026 --> 00:14:12.294
And all three of them, too.

00:14:12.628 --> 00:14:14.363
I have a bad feeling about this.

00:14:14.630 --> 00:14:16.598
This may be a worst case scenario.

00:14:17.099 --> 00:14:18.667
A worst case scenario?

00:14:18.901 --> 00:14:21.003
Could it be… something about the case.…

00:14:21.537 --> 00:14:22.338
No, that's not it.

00:14:23.872 --> 00:14:27.676
What I'm saying is without the knives
we won't be able make dinner.

00:14:27.676 --> 00:14:28.877
Geez.

00:14:28.877 --> 00:14:31.580
No way! We're not going to have dinner?

00:14:31.647 --> 00:14:33.248
What'd you get us all so worked up for?

00:14:33.515 --> 00:14:35.884
Shut up! This is of the utmost importance to me!

00:14:35.951 --> 00:14:37.353
That's right!

00:14:37.419 --> 00:14:38.354
All of you…

00:14:39.088 --> 00:14:40.356
Ah, Sensei!

00:14:40.789 --> 00:14:41.757
What's the matter?

00:14:42.358 --> 00:14:45.027
Who did that to the portrait in the hallway?

00:14:45.461 --> 00:14:46.662
The portrait…?

00:14:50.265 --> 00:14:52.101
Holmes' picture has been cut in half?

00:14:52.301 --> 00:14:53.736
Who would do such a thing?

00:14:54.136 --> 00:14:57.539
That knife… It's one of the three
that was in the kitchen.

00:14:57.840 --> 00:14:59.541
What a terrible prank to pull.

00:15:00.109 --> 00:15:01.543
None of you did this?

00:15:01.744 --> 00:15:03.278
Of course not!

00:15:03.379 --> 00:15:06.015
But it doesn't look like anyone
else could have either…

00:15:06.148 --> 00:15:07.616
You don't believe us?

00:15:07.716 --> 00:15:09.318
You think we're all just kids…

00:15:09.351 --> 00:15:11.320
No, that's not it, I'm…

00:15:11.854 --> 00:15:13.088
Katagiri-Sensei!

00:15:13.288 --> 00:15:15.624
Do you know where the rooftop key is?

00:15:16.825 --> 00:15:20.029
I thought it would be in the maintenance
room, but it's nowhere to be found.

00:15:20.362 --> 00:15:23.065
I need to use it for tomorrow's crime-scene
inspection, you see.

00:15:23.298 --> 00:15:24.533
This is bad…

00:15:25.401 --> 00:15:29.104
Well we've no choice but to call the academy
and get a duplicate key from them.

00:15:29.405 --> 00:15:32.207
But Maki-Sensei, in this weather…?

00:15:32.608 --> 00:15:34.576
It should be fine if I call them right now.

00:15:35.010 --> 00:15:38.614
The forecast says that it'll only become
really bad just after noon tomorrow.

00:15:38.881 --> 00:15:40.983
Until then, it should be alright.

00:15:42.885 --> 00:15:47.389
What was done to this picture and the
two knives that are still missing…

00:15:48.123 --> 00:15:49.758
and now the missing key.

00:15:50.225 --> 00:15:53.328
Who's doing this... and why?

00:16:21.921 --> 00:16:23.790
Sorry I'm late.

00:16:24.624 --> 00:16:26.693
The wind was stronger than I thought…

00:16:27.394 --> 00:16:28.862
Here, Katagiri-Sensei.

00:16:29.195 --> 00:16:30.497
I'll leave the key with you.

00:16:30.630 --> 00:16:31.398
Alright.

00:16:33.166 --> 00:16:35.902
But then, who took the key out in the first place?

00:16:36.836 --> 00:16:39.039
We're already past the planned time…

00:16:39.372 --> 00:16:42.342
So let us move over to the crime scene
before it starts pounding outside.

00:16:43.610 --> 00:16:46.479
And now, our special extra-curricular
training, this mystery…

00:16:47.013 --> 00:16:50.784
Our stage is an asylum on a deserted island
and our case is Jack the Ripper…

00:16:51.384 --> 00:16:54.287
…and the Riddle of the Closed
Room, which begins now.

00:16:56.523 --> 00:17:00.360
During the war, the asylum on this island
was used to house prisoners of war…

00:17:00.393 --> 00:17:03.163
Primarily those from England.

00:17:04.330 --> 00:17:08.468
But just before the end of the war,
when it took a turn for the worse…

00:17:08.635 --> 00:17:10.637
The custodians abandoned their mission…

00:17:10.804 --> 00:17:13.273
And escaped the island taking
the prisoners with them.

00:17:14.374 --> 00:17:19.746
When they left, ten prisoners who couldn't
fit onto the boat were left stranded.

00:17:21.181 --> 00:17:24.818
While waiting for a boat or ship to come
by, the ten who were left behind…

00:17:25.118 --> 00:17:29.289
…supported each another and lived
on despite the shortage of food.

00:17:30.056 --> 00:17:34.861
But as the days passed, they all
gradually gave in to despair.

00:17:35.462 --> 00:17:37.197
Nobody will ever come for us!

00:17:37.330 --> 00:17:39.833
We're all going to starve to death here!

00:17:39.966 --> 00:17:41.034
Then one day…

00:17:41.434 --> 00:17:44.637
Everyone, come here! A boat! There's a boat!

00:17:44.771 --> 00:17:45.605
What did you say?

00:17:46.606 --> 00:17:49.309
The boat that washed ashore had
been considerably damaged.

00:17:49.743 --> 00:17:53.813
The men, filled with renewed hope, scraped
together tools and began to repair it.

00:17:55.348 --> 00:18:00.253
But during the repairs, they came to realize
there was one terrible problem.

00:18:00.987 --> 00:18:01.821
And that was…

00:18:02.455 --> 00:18:04.224
Although ten men had been left behind…

00:18:04.591 --> 00:18:08.595
The boat, no matter what they
might do, could only fit seven.

00:18:09.028 --> 00:18:11.564
So three people had to stay behind on the island?

00:18:12.265 --> 00:18:12.966
That's correct.

00:18:13.299 --> 00:18:14.801
Even if they swam away from the island…

00:18:15.101 --> 00:18:18.304
They could only come ashore on
the hostile Japanese mainland.

00:18:18.838 --> 00:18:20.874
It was very unlikely for them to be rescued.

00:18:21.574 --> 00:18:23.877
Those that were left behind would sooner or later…

00:18:24.043 --> 00:18:26.279
…die of starvation and become rat food.

00:18:27.347 --> 00:18:31.317
And with this cold realization hanging
in the air among them…

00:18:31.384 --> 00:18:33.019
The first killing took place.

00:18:35.388 --> 00:18:39.726
One of the men was mercilessly hacked to pieces.

00:18:41.060 --> 00:18:44.964
And next to the murdered prisoner there
were letters written in blood…

00:18:45.799 --> 00:18:49.369
The name of a murderer very familiar
to those English people…

00:18:49.669 --> 00:18:51.738
"Jack the Ripper."

00:18:54.140 --> 00:18:57.243
The prisoners were desperately
afraid of further killings.

00:18:57.677 --> 00:18:59.379
And true enough, the very next day…

00:19:00.180 --> 00:19:03.283
The second victim's cruelly hacked-apart
upper body was discovered.

00:19:04.651 --> 00:19:08.555
If one more person is killed, those
who are left will fit in the boat.

00:19:10.423 --> 00:19:12.792
And finally, when the boat
was about to be finished…

00:19:13.092 --> 00:19:15.862
Their words came true and there was a third killing.

00:19:16.129 --> 00:19:19.232
The one that took place in the closed room.

00:19:22.535 --> 00:19:24.804
Before you go upstairs…

00:19:25.371 --> 00:19:28.107
I'm going to take this elevator to the
rooftop and wait for you in the hall.

00:19:34.981 --> 00:19:36.349
Here we go, at last.

00:19:36.983 --> 00:19:39.986
This storeroom is the closed room
where murder took place.

00:19:44.290 --> 00:19:45.024
Oh no!

00:19:47.493 --> 00:19:49.362
I see purple and pink rabbits.

00:19:51.064 --> 00:19:51.998
Owwww!

00:19:51.998 --> 00:19:53.399
You didn't have to say it out load!

00:19:54.300 --> 00:19:57.537
I didn't ask you all to come here so I could scare you.

00:19:58.304 --> 00:20:03.009
Everything I've just told you are facts that were
meticulously gathered and reproduced…

00:20:03.109 --> 00:20:07.413
…under my supervision by the
Detective Academy staff.

00:20:08.381 --> 00:20:12.285
Using that information and whatever you
gather from seeing the crime scene…

00:20:12.518 --> 00:20:15.922
I want you to solve this case and
the riddle of the closed room.

00:20:16.422 --> 00:20:17.056
Alright?

00:20:17.056 --> 00:20:18.791
Yes!

00:20:18.791 --> 00:20:20.426
Y-You're one enthusiastic kid.

00:20:21.160 --> 00:20:24.697
Now, its from this wooden shed built on the roof…

00:20:24.831 --> 00:20:27.333
…that the question of the two locked
doors of the closed room arises.

00:20:27.867 --> 00:20:30.203
Before we proceed, please take out the
diagram that was distributed to you.

00:20:32.472 --> 00:20:34.574
The interior layout of the storeroom is as you see it.

00:20:34.941 --> 00:20:38.845
The condition inside has been reproduced
as best as it could be.

00:20:39.445 --> 00:20:43.683
And this door has been securely locked
just like it was during the incident.

00:20:44.884 --> 00:20:46.286
This is the key to that lock.

00:20:46.819 --> 00:20:49.289
It's the duplicate key that we
had to make this morning.

00:20:49.822 --> 00:20:55.161
Unfortunately, we have no idea where the
original key to this room has gone.

00:20:56.262 --> 00:20:56.963
Sensei.

00:20:57.664 --> 00:20:58.998
What is it, Amakusa-kun?

00:20:59.866 --> 00:21:03.169
About the closed room murder that
occurred inside this storeroom…

00:21:04.170 --> 00:21:06.406
Is it not possible that the culprit used
a duplicate key like this one…

00:21:06.439 --> 00:21:09.008
…and made it look as if it was a closed room?

00:21:09.809 --> 00:21:11.377
That's a very plausible question.

00:21:11.844 --> 00:21:15.048
However, this used to be an armory back then.

00:21:15.581 --> 00:21:19.352
This key has a complicated design that was
made by a skilled military craftsman.

00:21:19.452 --> 00:21:22.622
This was to prevent the prisoners from
breaking in and stealing the weapons.

00:21:23.389 --> 00:21:27.160
At any rate, even with the advanced scientific
technology at the academy…

00:21:27.427 --> 00:21:30.863
…it took us many days to produce this duplicate key.

00:21:31.230 --> 00:21:35.868
It'd be impossible for the prisoners
of that time to have made a copy.

00:21:35.868 --> 00:21:37.604
I see.

00:21:38.004 --> 00:21:41.040
Now then, let's go inside the storeroom.

00:21:46.245 --> 00:21:49.682
These wooden crates are also arranged
approximately how they were back then.

00:21:55.355 --> 00:21:57.256
And that's the second door.

00:21:57.724 --> 00:22:01.627
The key used to unlock it is
the same as the first door.

00:22:07.967 --> 00:22:10.136
There's one window inside the closed room.

00:22:10.670 --> 00:22:13.206
It looks like a single adult could get through it.

00:22:13.906 --> 00:22:15.675
Even if someone were to be
able to get out this way…

00:22:16.242 --> 00:22:18.344
All that awaits him below is a steep cliff.

00:22:21.014 --> 00:22:21.948
Hey, take a look at this!

00:22:22.548 --> 00:22:24.384
They're really detailed…

00:22:24.650 --> 00:22:28.187
Even bothering to reproduce "Jack
the Ripper" in letters of blood.

00:22:29.122 --> 00:22:29.789
What?

00:22:30.590 --> 00:22:33.893
Katagiri-kun, you even had that reproduced?

00:22:34.260 --> 00:22:37.096
N-No, I didn't ask for that.

00:22:37.563 --> 00:22:38.531
What did you say?

00:22:39.098 --> 00:22:41.200
Eh, what is this, I wonder.

00:22:42.602 --> 00:22:44.470
K-Kyuu!

00:22:48.674 --> 00:22:49.442
This is...

00:23:05.291 --> 00:23:07.393
S-Saburoumaru-san!

00:23:09.929 --> 00:23:13.332
The sudden appearance of the left-half
of Saburoumaru-san…

00:23:14.667 --> 00:23:20.073
This same horrible event that happened half
a century ago… It's been replicated.

00:23:21.574 --> 00:23:26.012
And as for the rest of us who were shaking
in terror, not one of us has any idea…

00:23:26.546 --> 00:23:30.650
…where the missing right half went or
what the meaning of the two locks is.

00:23:36.600 --> 00:23:47.530
Unable to cry in front of you, I'm whistling instead.

00:23:50.240 --> 00:24:02.090
Saying "I'll always be beside you" without saying a single word.

00:24:02.090 --> 00:24:09.180
My loving heart is overflowing; those sad thoughts...

00:24:09.180 --> 00:24:14.810
are like things that fade away unnoticed in mid-air.

00:24:15.010 --> 00:24:21.020
And again, with a mighty smile of relief...

00:24:21.150 --> 00:24:26.570
I will see you off.

00:24:26.820 --> 00:24:36.410
Goodbye and thank you.

00:24:37.016 --> 00:24:39.619
Over the course of time, the slasher is revealed.

00:24:39.919 --> 00:24:41.821
Even with the island's mystery
hideously colored in red…

00:24:41.988 --> 00:24:43.656
…one of us still seems to have a hidden purpose.

00:24:43.856 --> 00:24:48.361
Is the key to solving the trick of the closed room's
two locks within the answer derived by Kazuma?

00:24:48.861 --> 00:24:53.332
But as our investigations are being
ridiculed, a second killing happens

00:24:53.966 --> 00:24:55.268
There's only one answer!

00:24:57.036 --> 00:24:59.672
The murderer may appear anytime.

00:25:00.006 --> 00:25:00.940
Be careful.

00:25:05.645 --> 00:25:08.181
Haha, it's just Saburoumaru-san's ghost!
